{
  "_description": "Claude Code auto-approve permissions for safe, read-only operations. Merged into ~/.claude/settings.json during installation. See trusted-domains.json for the WebFetch domain allowlist.",

  "_hardening": {
    "_note": "v3.17.0 Phase 1.1 rescoped this baseline to be read-only at the side-effect level, not the command-name level. Matcher evidence and the two empirical findings are in docs/releases/v3/v3.17/development/permission-matcher-findings.md. Enforced by scripts/validate_permission_baseline.py.",
    "_reference_pattern_bash": "Bash(sed -n *) - admits the read-only -n form while excluding sed -i.",
    "_reference_pattern_powershell": "PowerShell(Get-CimInstance -ClassName Win32_OperatingSystem) - pins the class so no trailing wildcard can reach -CimSession or -ComputerName.",
    "removed_v3_17_0": {
      "Bash(find), Bash(find *)": "Admits -fprintf, -fprint, -fls, -execdir and -ok, which write files or execute. Claude Code's built-in read-only set already auto-approves safe find with better analysis, so removal is coverage-neutral.",
      "Bash(cat), Bash(cat *), Bash(echo), Bash(echo *), Bash(printf *)": "Output redirection turns each into an arbitrary write, and > truncates the target regardless of what the command emits. All are in Claude Code's built-in read-only set, which analyses redirects properly, so the glob entry only widened the grant.",
      "Bash(sort *)": "sort -o FILE writes. Official docs name sort as a write-capable-flag command. Bare Bash(sort) is retained for pipeline use.",
      "Bash(awk *)": "An awk program can write via BEGIN{print > \"/path\"}. Program text is arbitrary, so no safe rescope exists.",
      "Bash(gh api *)": "Admits --method POST|PATCH|PUT|DELETE, so it could delete a repository or close pull requests. The specific gh pr/issue/repo/run/release read entries retain the useful coverage.",
      "Bash(git branch *)": "Admits git branch -D and -m, which delete and rename branches. Replaced by pinned list, -a, -r, -v, --show-current, --contains, --merged and --no-merged forms.",
      "Bash(git tag *)": "Admits git tag -d and bare tag creation. Replaced by -l, --list, --contains and --points-at.",
      "Bash(git remote *)": "Admits git remote add, remove, rename and set-url. Replaced by -v, show and get-url.",
      "Bash(ip *)": "Admits ip link set and ip addr add, which mutate network state. Replaced by explicit show subcommands.",
      "Bash(sysctl *)": "Admits sysctl -w, which mutates kernel parameters. Replaced by -a and -n.",
      "Bash(ss *)": "Admits ss -K, which kills established sockets. Replaced by pinned read flags.",
      "Bash(journalctl *)": "Admits --vacuum-size, --vacuum-time and --rotate, which delete logs. Replaced by -n, -u, --since and --no-pager.",
      "Bash(dmesg *)": "Admits -C and --clear, which wipe the kernel ring buffer. Replaced by -T and --level.",
      "Bash(xcrun *)": "xcrun executes an arbitrary named tool, making it an unstripped exec wrapper. Replaced by --find and --show-sdk-* query forms.",
      "Bash(xcode-select *)": "Admits --switch and --install, which mutate the active toolchain. Replaced by -p and --print-path.",
      "PowerShell(Get-CimInstance *), PowerShell(Get-WmiObject *)": "Dual-mode: returned objects expose mutating methods (Win32_Process Create), and the trailing wildcard admits -CimSession and -ComputerName, converting a local read into a remote one. Replaced by class-pinned reads.",
      "PowerShell(Test-Connection *), PowerShell(Test-NetConnection *), PowerShell(Resolve-DnsName *)": "Arbitrary outbound network reach to any host or DNS server from an auto-approved pattern, which is inconsistent with the strict WebFetch domain allowlist in this same baseline and usable as an exfiltration channel. Removed deliberately; recorded as a decision rather than an oversight."
    },
    "retained_with_rationale": {
      "PowerShell alias entries (gci, gc, sls, select, sort, ...)": "Redundant because Claude Code canonicalizes aliases before matching, but retained because canonicalization coverage may vary by version and sub-task 1.1 forbids reducing coverage.",
      "Bash(xargs *) entries": "Redundant because bare xargs is stripped before matching, retained on the same version-safety reasoning.",
      "Print and filter patterns carrying a trailing wildcard": "Their only exposure is the UNVERIFIED redirect question, which applies uniformly to every wildcard pattern in both shells rather than to these entries specifically. Singling them out would be security theatre. See finding 2 of the findings document."
    }
  },

